  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n   The central idea is that Erd\u0151s discrepancy minimization can be viewed as a problem of aligning measure. The goal is to find a step function h(x) on [0,2] with integral 1 that minimizes the maximum overlap with its shifted versions. This is achieved through a constrained optimization over discrete representations of h, using active-set methods to reduce the worst-case overlaps. The bottleneck in existing methods is local convergence, so the plan introduces multi-scale optimization across different resolutions and structured initializations to escape local optima.\n\n   Rather than incremental improvements, we take a principled shift: \n   - Use different resolutions (filled with structured candidate functions)\n   - Apply coordinate-wise updates with reflected gradients for imbalance\n   - Incorporate harmonic analysis conversions (Fourier-domain) for smoothness constraints\n   - Use exponential penalty transforms for non-smooth constraints\n\n   This differs from existing variable-projection methods by:\n   - Rigorous aliasing control in shifted evaluations\n   - Constraint satisfaction via L2 projection instead of ad-hoc clipping\n   - Dynamic resolution expansion based on gradient information\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n   We represent h as a balanced sequence of n equidistant samples:\n   h_i = score(x_i) for x_i = (i+0.5)dx, dx=2/n\n\n   Exact objective:\n   C5 = (2/n) * max_{k=0}^{n-1} [ sum_{i} | i_noi | ] but note: we use the integral approximation via the correlation method\n\n   In discrete form with grid dx:\n   F(h) = (dx * max_k np.correlate(h,1-h,mode='full')[k])\n\n   Subject to:\n   - 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 \u2200i\n   - \u03a3 h_i dx = 1 \u21d2 \u03a3 h_i = n/2\n\n   The aliasing error for shifts is controlled by:\n   E(k) = ||h_|| / ||1-h_|| is bounded by |k|/n < 1/2 to prevent domain wrapping effects\n\n   We parametrize the optimization vector in unconstrained form:\n   z_i in \u211d with pmap h(z) = \u03c3(z) / (\u03c3(z) + \u03c4(z)) for logistic projection\n\n   The error penalty used is:\n   E_penalty = max_k [sum_i h_i * (1-h_{i+k}) ] + \u03bb * ||\u03a3 h_i - n/2|| \n\n   This enables gradient propagation and avoids constraint violations. The solver handles:\n   - Lower dimensions (\u2264 800 points) with sparse derivatives\n   - Full resolution (up to 10,000 points) with dense-vector approximations\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   a. Initialization phase (30 seconds)\n   - If parent available, reproject to n=200 using cubic interpolation\n   - Generate five structured candidates:\n        * Thue-Morse balance\n        * Sine wave variations (freq=0.2,0.4,0.6)\n        * Champernowne irreversible sequence\n        * Golden ratio optimized binary\n        *Maximin window placement\n\n   b. Active-set optimization (main loop)\n   - For each candidate, perform:\n        * Gradient-based descent (line search adaptive)\n        * Constraint violation repair via SLSQP\n        * Multi-vector projection (L2 projection of constraints)\n\n   The algorithm alternates between phases:\n   Active reduction (target 90% reduction in worst overlaps)\n   \u2192 Resolution refinement (-10 to 80% scale)\n   \u2192 Structure enforcement (Fourier smoothing)\n\n   The objective gradient is computed as:\n        \u2202F/\u2202z_i = 2 * exponential terms * (excess schedule shift)\n\n   and propagation is done via automatic differentiation.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n   - Default optimization range: \u03bb in [1e-4,1e3], penalty adjustment every 100 descents\n   - Candidate set: currently uses multinomial sequences. Alternatively try:\n        * Walsh functions for dyadic representations\n        * Chebyshev interpolation after solving in Fourier domain\n   - The sine wave method produces improveable behavior (tested but not deployed). The Thue-Morse array should be initialized with golden ratio-like properties.\n\n   The implementation will need to spawn multiple processes (currently single-core) if memory permits.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n   - The optimized run should work within 600 seconds at optimal resolution (n=1 to 800)\n   - Local linear extrapolation for resolutions under 100 points\n   - Progressive dimension increase with early termination\n   - Robust parameter sets based on L2 condition numbers\n\n   The fallback implementation handles:\n   - Invalid initializations by gracefully converting binary to continuous via \u0141omnicki projection\n   - Profile-based resource allocation if multiple cores were available\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n   - Final solution must satisfy: 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 and \u03a3h_i = n/2\n   - Re-evaluation using scipy.correlate, mode='full'\n   - Score based on grid-overlap integrals only\n   - Wraps for large n values (n > 1e6) with optimized tensor products\n\n   The final representation is three arrays: \n   h_values (flattened), c5_bound (scalar), n_points (integer)",
